# Mobile Styling

**StyleSheet.create + theme tokens.** No Tailwind, no styled-components/RN. (Future: NativeWind is an option if alignment with FE Tailwind becomes worth it.)

## Theme tokens

`src/constants/theme.ts` is the single source for color/spacing/typography tokens. The **values come from the ticket's confirmed Figma design tokens** (cached under `.agentic/plans/<TICKET>/figma/` by `/start`) — derive them from the design; never invent them, and never leave a framework starter's defaults in place. The shape:

```ts
// src/constants/theme.ts — token NAMES are the convention; VALUES come from the
// confirmed Figma variables (every token needs both a light and a dark value)
import { Platform } from 'react-native';

export const Colors = {
  light: {
    text: '<figma text/primary>',
    background: '<figma surface/default>',
    tint: '<figma brand/accent>',          // the brand accent drives links, selection, CTAs
    icon: '<figma icon/muted>',
    tabIconDefault: '<figma icon/muted>',
    tabIconSelected: '<figma brand/accent>',
    border: '<figma border/default>',
    error: '<figma feedback/error>',
  },
  dark: {
    // same token names, the design's dark values — never reuse light values here
  },
};
```

> ⚠ **ANTI-example — the Expo starter palette.** A `theme.ts` still carrying the starter stub (the giveaway is the default accent `tint: '#0a7ea4'`) is an UNBRANDED design system: `/start`'s foundation check, the explorer, and `design-parity` all treat that exact state as a BLOCKING gap on UI tickets. (THIS repo's `theme.ts` is currently in that state — replace it via a foundation/design-system task derived from Figma; never copy the starter values into new work as if they were house style.)

```ts
// Branded fonts load via expo-font and slot in here; the Platform.select shape below
// is the system-font FALLBACK structure, not a statement that system fonts are the brand.
export const Fonts = Platform.select({
  ios: {
    sans: 'system-ui',
    serif: 'ui-serif',
    rounded: 'ui-rounded',
    mono: 'ui-monospace',
  },
  default: {
    sans: 'normal',
    serif: 'serif',
    rounded: 'normal',
    mono: 'monospace',
  },
});

// Spacing/Radius scale VALUES are e.g. (THIS repo) illustrations — confirm against
// the design's spacing tokens; the convention is the named scale, not these numbers.
export const Spacing = {
  xs: 4,
  sm: 8,
  md: 12,
  lg: 16,
  xl: 24,
  '2xl': 32,
} as const;

export const Radius = {
  sm: 4,
  md: 8,
  lg: 12,
  xl: 16,
  full: 9999,
} as const;
```

## `useThemeColor` hook

```ts
// src/hooks/use-theme-color.ts
import { useColorScheme } from './use-color-scheme';
import { Colors } from '@/constants/theme';

type ColorName = keyof typeof Colors.light & keyof typeof Colors.dark;

export function useThemeColor(
  props: { light?: string; dark?: string },
  colorName: ColorName,
) {
  const theme = useColorScheme() ?? 'light';
  return props[theme] ?? Colors[theme][colorName];
}
```

Consumers read by token, with optional overrides:

```tsx
const color = useThemeColor({}, 'text');
const tint = useThemeColor({ light: '#1565c0', dark: '#90caf9' }, 'tint');
```

## StyleSheet at module scope

Always define styles outside the component to avoid re-creation on every render. Module scope can't react to theme changes, so the split is: **structure (layout, spacing, type sizes) in the StyleSheet; colors resolved per-theme at render** — via the themed primitives, or `useThemeColor` composed in a style array. Never pin a theme (`Colors.light.*`) or a hex literal in a module-scope StyleSheet — that breaks dark mode for every consumer.

```tsx
// ✅ — structure at module scope, colors from the theme at render
const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: { flex: 1, padding: Spacing.lg },
  title: { fontSize: 20, fontWeight: 'bold' },
});

export function Card({ children }: { children: ReactNode }) {
  return (
    <ThemedView style={styles.container}>
      <ThemedText type="subtitle" style={styles.title}>Card title</ThemedText>
      {children}
    </ThemedView>
  );
}

// ✅ — when a themed primitive doesn't fit, compose useThemeColor in a style array
export function Badge({ label }: { label: string }) {
  const background = useThemeColor({}, 'tint');
  return <View style={[styles.container, { backgroundColor: background }]}>...</View>;
}

// ❌ — created on every render
export function Card() {
  const styles = StyleSheet.create({ container: { ... } });
  return <View style={styles.container} />;
}

// ❌ — theme pinned at module scope: ignores dark mode
const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: { flex: 1, backgroundColor: '#fff' },
  title: { color: Colors.light.text },
});
```

## Dynamic styles

For values that genuinely depend on props or theme, compose arrays:

```tsx
const color = useThemeColor({}, 'text');

<Text style={[styles.text, { color }]}>Hello</Text>
```

Avoid inline style objects with literal values — those should go in the StyleSheet.

## Platform-specific values

Use `Platform.select` for cross-platform differences:

```ts
const headerHeight = Platform.select({ ios: 44, android: 56, default: 50 });

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  header: {
    height: headerHeight,
    paddingTop: Platform.OS === 'ios' ? 20 : 0,
  },
});
```

For larger divergences, use platform-specific files — see [platform-files.md](./platform-files.md).

## Responsive sizing

For layouts that scale to different screens:

```ts
import { useWindowDimensions } from 'react-native';

const { width, height } = useWindowDimensions();
const isTablet = width >= 768;
```

For typography scaling, use `PixelRatio.getFontScale()` — respect the user's accessibility settings.

## Light / dark mode

`useColorScheme()` from `@/hooks/use-color-scheme` returns `'light' | 'dark' | null`. Components consume via `useThemeColor`. The themed primitives (`<ThemedText>`, `<ThemedView>`) wrap this so you rarely call `useThemeColor` directly.

Default to system theme; allow user override stored in AsyncStorage if needed.

## Hard rules

- ❌ Inline `StyleSheet.create()` inside component body
- ❌ Hex literals scattered through components — use `Colors` or `useThemeColor`
- ❌ `style={{ ...obj, ...override }}` — use `style={[base, override]}` arrays
- ❌ `Dimensions.get('window').width` in render — use `useWindowDimensions` (responds to rotation)
- ❌ Platform checks inside `render` for non-trivial divergence — use platform-specific files instead
